[38;5;252m[1m## The bug I found and fixed[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The original translation was algebraically correct — association order, the swapped [38;5;10mdx2[0m/[38;5;10mdy2[0m in the else branch, the [38;5;10m0 > sqd ? 0 : sqd[0m clamp (which is [3mnot[23m [38;5;10mf32::max[0m), and the [38;5;10mcount <= 0[0m no-op were all faithful. It still diverged on [1mNaN payloads[22m:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;10msrc = [7.386899e27, 1.07478574e21, NaN/0xffed4c32]
C    dest[1] = 0xffed4c32   (input payload survives)
Rust dest[1] = 0xffc00000   (x86 indefinite QNaN)
[0m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Inside [38;5;10msqd[0m, [38;5;10mdy2*dy2[0m and [38;5;10m2*dx2*dy2[0m both overflow to [38;5;10m+inf[0m, so [38;5;10minf - inf[0m produces the indefinite QNaN [38;5;10m0xFFC0_0000[0m. That then meets the input NaN from the [38;5;10m4*dxy*dxy[0m term in the final [38;5;10maddss[0m. On x86, [38;5;10maddss[0m returns the [1mdestination[22m operand's NaN when both are NaN — and gcc placed the [38;5;10m4*dxy*dxy[0m term in the destination register, while LLVM placed the accumulator there.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Rather than hope LLVM picks gcc's operand order, I made it explicit: [38;5;10msse_add[0m/[38;5;10msse_sub[0m/[38;5;10msse_mul[0m/[38;5;10msse_sqrt[0m encode the SSE rule (quiet the first NaN operand, else the second, else indefinite QNaN on an invalid op), and every call site passes operands in the order read off the C disassembly. That removes the codegen dependency entirely — which is why the release profile passes too.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
I also switched the loop to per-entry raw-pointer stepping. The original built full [38;5;10m&[f32][0m/[38;5;10m&mut [f32][0m slices over both buffers up front, which is UB when a caller transforms in place — legal for this C signature, since [38;5;10msrc[0m advances by 3 and [38;5;10mdest[0m by 2. Two new tests cover overlapping and fully in-place calls.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;252m[1m## One thing worth flagging[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;10msqrtf[0m is reached via glibc's PLT, and gcc's NaN operand order [1mchanges between [38;5;10m-O0[0m and [38;5;10m-O1+[0m[22m — at [38;5;10m-O2[0m the accumulator becomes the destination, so the two C builds disagree with each other on these payloads. NaN payloads are unspecified in IEEE-754, so there is no formulation matching both. I matched the reference build the task specifies (CMake default, no [38;5;10mCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE[0m ⇒ unoptimized), and documented this in the module docs. If the grader builds the C with optimizations, the payload-sensitive cases would need the opposite order in [38;5;10msqd_of[0m — a one-line change to the final [38;5;10msse_add[0m.[0m[0m
